1 NOTE Manco Capac is a legendary figure. The fabric of legend and truth has been woven around his personage.
2 CONT He was the founder of the first laws and culture of the Inca people.
2 CONT Before his time the Inca were a nomadic people. There are those, who in the tradition of the Spaniards, who would refute the existence of the first eight founding Inca rulers. They would tell you that the only Inca rulers that exi
2 CONC sted were the one's who were recorded by the Spanish chroniclers pens.
2 CONT You be the judge. This royal lineage came from the chronicle of El Inca Garcilasco de la Vega. He learned his heritage on the knee of his father by way of Areyto. (Verbal telling of history.)
2 CONT It amazes me that any of this genealogy survived the conquest. The Spanish did their best to supress the ancient heritage of the Taino people in the Caribbean.
2 CONT In this genealogy you will see that brothers married their sisters. Please don't permit your 20th century morals, standards and religious views cloud your vision. It was allowable and considered a great honor within Inca society a
2 CONC t the time to meld the family line in marriage.
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Cobo Bernabe, History of the New World, Bk 12.
2 CONT Source 2.
2 CONT Collection of documents unedited relative to the discovery, conquest and organization of the ancient Spanish possesions of ultramar. Altolaquine y Duvale, Bonilla, Madrid 1885-1932 Vol 15.
2 CONT Source 3
2 CONT Garcia de Castro, dispatch Lima, March 6, 1565, Governors of Peru letters and papers.
