1 NOTE An Interesting note:
2 CONT Tupac Inca Yupanqui explored the Pacific Islands for ten months. He made landfall on the Galapagos Islands, Polynesia and Easter Island where he set up a kingship for a short time before leaving there.
2 CONT The Source for this story is Pedro Sarmiento de Gamboa in 1572 who wrote a book, History of the Incas.
2 CONT Some speculate that Easter islanders have an oral tradition of a group of long eared Hanauepe coming to the island from a place that was unknown. Easter Island genealogy metions a Tupa Ariki, prince king Tupa who ruled around 1485
2 CONC .
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Cobo Bernabe, History of the New World, Bk 12.
2 CONT Source 2.
2 CONT Collection of documents unedited relative to the discovery, conquest and organization of the ancient Spanish possesions of ultramar. Altolaquine y Duvale, Bonilla, Madrid 1885-1932 Vol 15.
2 CONT Source 3
2 CONT Garcia de Castro, dispatch Lima, March 6, 1565, Governors of Peru letters and papers.
