2 CONT 14. TOMAS4 SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA (TOMAS3 SANCHEZ-DURAN, FRANCISCO2 SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA, JUAN1 SANCHEZ-Y-ORTEGA) was born April 23, 1680 in Sagrario Metropolitano, Monterrey, Nuevo Leon, Mexico, and died Abt. 1739. '''He married 
2 CONC MARIA-JOSEFA DE-LA-GARZA-SOSA December 12, 1707 in Sagrario Metropolitano, Monterrey, Nuevo Leon, Mexico, daughter of JUAN DE-LA-GARZA-MONTEMAYOR and MARIA DE SOSA. She was born 1690.''' Notes for TOMAS SANCHEZ-DE-LA-BARRERA:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mil Familias III, by Rodolfo Gonzalez de la Garza. Page 81.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for MARIA-JOSEFA DE-LA-GARZA-SOSA:
2 CONT
2 CONT She is a descendent of the Onate-Zaldivar Family of Zacatecas and colonizers of New Mexico. Another participant in the colonization of New Mexico (USA), and an ancestor of this group is Bernabé de las Casas, who married Dona Beatr
2 CONC iz Navarro, the daughter of Don Juan Navarro, a founder of Saltillo. Beatriz was the first wife of Captain Alonso de Sosa Albornoz, killed in New Mexico by orders of Oñate. Alonso’s grandfather was the Conquistador Don Andrés de T
2 CONC apia, perhaps the most trustworthy and reliable of the captains of the Marquis del Valle de Oaxaca, Hernán Cortés. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A History of New Mexico by Gaspar Perez de Villagra, Alcala - 1610, translated by Gilberto Espinosa.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She is a descendent of the Onate-Zaldivar Family of Zacatecas and colonizers of New Mexico. Another participant in the colonization of New Mexico (USA), and an ancestor of this group is Bernabé de las Casas, who married Dona Beatr
2 CONC iz Navarro, the daughter of Don Juan Navarro, a founder of Saltillo. Beatriz was the first wife of Captain Alonso de Sosa Albornoz, killed in New Mexico by orders of Oñate. Alonso’s grandfather was the Conquistador Don Andrés de T
2 CONC apia, perhaps the most trustworthy and reliable of the captains of the Marquis del Valle de Oaxaca, Hernán Cortés. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A History of New Mexico by Gaspar Perez de Villagra, Alcala - 1610, translated by Gilberto Espinosa.
2 CONT
